## 1. Introduction
1. IntroductionSustainable and renewable energy conversion and storage technologies are urgently desired and positively developed owing to the exhaustion of worldwide fossil fuels along with ever-growing environmental deterioration [1,2]. Among numerous candidates for mitigating the severe issue, electrocatalysis, especially in water splitting, fuel cells, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) reduction, etc. had exerted a tremendous fascination on environmental remediation and energy conversion fields thanks to its low energy consumption and easy-controlled electrochemical reaction pathways [3–6]. More significantly, the designment and fabrication of highly efficient and long-term electrocatalysts especially the state-of-art bifunctional or multifunctional electrocatalysts would promote the development of electrocatalysis technology, and further facilitate the construction of the next-generation energy conversion systems [7,8].
Interesting fact that the Pt-based nanomaterials especially commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) (Pt supported on activated carbon) are recognized as the efficient multi-functional electrocatalysts in some significant clean energy reactions [i.e. hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), alcohols oxidation reaction (AOR)], even though it is still insufficient due to low metal utilization efficiency and susceptibility to poisoning [9,10]. Among the abundant Pt nanostructures, closely packed assemblies of Pt nanoparticles which termed as nanoclusters could maximize the Pt active sites for above electrochemical reactions [11–13]. Recently, Zhang et al. constructed Pt nanoclusters anchored nitrogen-doped carbon to form the strong metal-support interaction for high-efficient HER and acquired a low overpotential of \(19.5~\mathrm{mV}\) and a low Tafel slope of \(40.8~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}\) [14]. Additionally, researchers combined spherical Pt nanoclusters with amino-clay/MWCNT supports as dual-functional electrocatalyst for ORR and AOR and demonstrated that the strong positive charges on Pt nanoclusters are favorable to enhance the electronic interaction of Pt-hybrid supports and thereby improving electrocatalytic performance [15]. More importantly, the passive carbon derivatives support generally facilitates the dispersion of Pt but not impart the electronic structure of active metal, still unable to accelerate the sluggish kinetics and promote the catalytic efficiency. Substantial reports demonstrate that the association of noble metals with versatile and active supports could acquire more active sites and produce synergistic effect via strong metal-support interface interaction [16–18]. Moreover, when combining with supporting materials, the (111) crystal plane of Pt is more active than other crystal face for electrocatalytic reactions [19,20]. For the sake of acquiring a high-effective Pt nanoclusters based electrocatalyst with exposed (111) crystal plane and further optimize its catalytic performance, the rational design and convenient fabrication of functional supports are rather essential.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a type of novel crystalline porous materials constructed by metal nodes and organic linkers, present structural stability, large surface area and adjustable electronic structure, had aroused the growing attention of researches and had explored in electrocatalytic fields [21–23]. Unfortunately, the defect of MOF, poor conductivity limited its electrocatalytic application [24]. To improve its conductivity, researchers focus on the design and preparation of two-dimensional (2D) semiconductive or conductive MOFs for electrochemical reaction [25,26]. Recently, a series of Co-Ni bimetallic MOF were prepared via surfactant-assisted strategy for methanol oxidation and overall water splitting, whereas the catalytic activity of MOR was only approximately \(10~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\) and the HER performance is significantly lower than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) [27]. Comparatively, the hybridization of MOF with high conductivity Pt metals would reduce preparation difficulty, establish the metal-supports elec
tronic interaction, and afford excellent electrocatalytic properties could be deemed as an alternative strategy for high-efficient electrocatalyst. For example, a novel Ni-MOF@Pt electrocatalyst was constructed via in situ growth to catalyze hydrogen evolution and achieved low overpotential of \(43/102~\mathrm{mV}\) in acid/alkali environment [28]. However, rare report on hybrid Pt/MOF as multifunctional electrocatalyst for HER and AOR with outstanding catalytic performance has been found. Of note, Fe-containing MOFs distinguishing from other MOFs owns wide distribution on earth (accounting for \(5.1\%\) of the crust) and has advantage in large-scale commercial hydrogen generation attributed to that the low-cost Fe foil could form self-supported MOF structure via in-situ growth afford extra performance [29]. MIL-100 (Fe), a thermodynamically stable Fe-based MOFs constructed by super tetrahedron, has high specific surface area, suitable pore size and accessible Fe active sites distributed in MOF backbone [30,31], which facilitates the adsorption/desorption process in the electrocatalysis and serve as a porous support for Pt nanoclusters for maximizing the atom utilization efficiency and electrocatalytic active sites. More interestingly, the strong interfacial interaction between Pt metals and defect-rich surface of MOFs may adjust the electronic structure and provide more active sites to improve the electrocatalytic performance [28,32].
Motivated by above lines, we prepared Pt nanoclusters embedded MIL-100(Fe) via a facile hydrothermal method and further fabricated the Pt/MIL-100(Fe) electrode for electrocatalytic HER and AOR. Impressively, the as-obtained electrode displays superior HER performance (an overpotential of \(25~\mathrm{mV}\) and a Tafel slope of \(19.7~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}\) in acidic electrolyte; an overpotential of \(29~\mathrm{mV}\) and a Tafel slope of \(37.8~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}\) in alkaline electrolyte). Furthermore, the electrode is also employed as the anode for ethylene glycol oxidation reaction (EGOR) and acquires the excellent mass activity of \(11826~\mathrm{mA~mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}\), which promotes the applications for direct alcohol full cells (DAFCs). It is noted that the electrocatalytic activity and long-term stability for HER and EGOR of Pt/MIL-100 (Fe) with low Pt loadings (10 wt%) catalyst is obviously superior than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) one, which is attributed to the higher surface-active sites, efficient electron transfer, and the strong interfacial interaction between Pt nanoclusters and MIL-100(Fe). The above investigation sheds light into the rational design of the state-of-the-art effective Pt/MOF electrocatalyst for electrochemical reaction and broads the applications for water splitting and fuel cells.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Physicochemical characterization of MIL-100(Fe) and Pt/MIL-100 (Fe)
2. Results and discussion2.1. Physicochemical characterization of MIL-100(Fe) and Pt/MIL-100 (Fe)The MIL-100(Fe) and Pt nanoclusters embedded MIL-100(Fe) was synthesized via a facile modified hydrothermal method and the detailed procedures were given in Supporting Information (SI). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM), FT-IR, and Raman spectrum were carried out to identify the morphology and chemical structure of MIL-100(Fe). TEM images display that MIL-100(Fe) has a rather regular octahedral with the dimensions of 250–400 nm (Fig. 1A). As shown in Fig. 1D, the broad band at \(3429~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) confirms the –OH bonds owing to the existence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) moieties. More importantly, the typical absorption peaks at 1380, 1447, 1572, and \(1626~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are ascribed to the asymmetric \((\nu_{s})\)/symmetric \((\nu_{s})\) vibrations of –COOH, and the difference value between \(\nu_{as}\) and \(\nu_{s}\) are 179 and \(192~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively [31], revealing the existence of coordination between carboxyl group and metal ion center. Moreover, the C–H stretching vibrations of benzene ring are peaked at 759 and \(709~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and the Fe-O bond is located at \(481~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) [33]. To further verify the MOFs structure,
Raman spectroscopy is measured and depicted in Fig. 1E. From the spectrum, the characteristic peak at \( 494\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) corresponds to Fe-O bond as well as lattice vibration. As for the organic component of MIL-100(Fe), the peaks located at 812, 1006, and \( 1607\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) are attributed to \( \mathsf{C{-}H} \) and \( \scriptstyle{C=C} \) bonds from benzene ring and residual peaks at 1374, 1470, \( 1539\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) are arisen from \( \nu_{as}/\nu_{s} \) of –COOH [34]. Above characterizations confirm the octahedral crystal structure and chemical information of MIL-100(Fe), indicating the successful preparation of this MOF. After depositing Pt, the closely Pt nanoparticles prefer to assemble into Pt nanoclusters and decorated on the MIL-100(Fe) surface (Fig. 1B), suggesting the efficacious dispersion for Pt nanoclusters on supporting materials surface. To meticulously study the lattice spacing and crystal plane orientation of Pt nanoclusters, the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) was performed. As shown in Fig. 1C and Fig. S1, the well-defined Pt nanoclusters with distinct boundaries and lattice fringes with the \( d \)-spacing values of \( 2.25\,\mathrm{\AA} \), which is in accordance with the (111) crystal plane of Pt face-centered cubic \( \left(f_{cc}\right) \) [35,36]. It is acknowledged that water molecules could efficaciously absorb on Pt active sites to form \( \mathrm{Pt-H_{ad}} \) and the (111) crystal plane of Pt is more active than other plane when combining with supporting materials, suggesting the dominant Pt (111) crystal plane in \( \mathrm{Pt}/ \) MIL-100(Fe) would promote the catalytic activity of HER and AOR [20,37]. Furthermore, the intense signals of C, Fe and Pt element from corresponding elemental mapping images (Fig. 1F) suggest the construction of Pt nanoclusters embedded MOF.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of MIL-100(Fe) and Pt/MIL-100 (Fe) were performed to acquire the crystalline information. As depicted in Fig. 2A, the diffraction peaks of as-prepared MIL-100 (Fe) are matched with the standard simulation XRD pattern and
reported literature [38,39]. After depositing Pt nanoclusters, the characteristic peaks of MOF in Pt/MIL-100 (Fe) sample are barely changed compared to those of the parent specimen, indicating the framework of MOF is well preserved. More importantly, the clear peak at \( 39.9^{\circ} \) in Pt/MIL-100 (Fe) corresponds to the (111) crystal face of Pt (JCPDS #01–1194) appears [40,41], which possesses superior catalytic activity than other lattice planes. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further measured to elucidate the chemical state and elemental composition of the specimens. As shown in Fig. 2B, the survey spectra indicate the existence of C, O and Fe elements in Mil-100(Fe) samples. Distinguished with the parent MIL-100(Fe), the appearance of Pt 4d and Pt 4f regions demonstrates the deposition of Pt nanoclusters on the MIL-100(Fe) surfaces. Fig. 2C exhibits the C 1 s high-resolution spectra of two specimens and the major peaks at 284.8, 286.5 and \( 288.7\,\mathrm{eV} \) are attributed to the organic ligands of Fe-MOFs [31]. For the case of Fe 2p spectra (Fig. 2D), it could be deconvoluted into six characteristic peaks, wherein the peaks located at 710.9, 714.6, 724.2 and \( 726.9\,\mathrm{eV} \) are ascribed to the \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) of Fe \( 2{\tt p}_{1/2} \) and Fe \( 2{\tt p}_{3/2} \). More importantly, the \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) species fingerprint peaks located at 718.7 and 731.3 eV confirm that \( \mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \) plays the predominant role in MIL-100 (Fe) [42,43]. After depositing of Pt nanoclusters, the significant deviation of major peaks location is not occurred for Pt/MIL-100(Fe) sample, suggesting the well-maintained original MOF frame structure. As above discussed, the C 1 s and Fe 2p of MIL-100(Fe) display no significant shift through the hybridization of Pt nanoclusters, it is deduced that Pt may embedded to oxygen-containing groups especially – COOH on the MIL-100(Fe) surface, which can be verified by the emerging Pt-O peak in high-resolution 1 s spectra (Fig. 2E). For
the case of MIL-100(Fe), the deconvoluted peaks assigned to \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}\) and Fe-O are located at 531.8 and \(533.7\;\mathrm{eV}.\) . After embedded with Pt nanoclusters, the chemical environments of \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O}\) and Fe-O changed and result from the slight decrease of binding energy. More significantly, an emerging peak located at \(530.8\ \mathrm{\eV}\) could be ascribed to Pt-O interaction between Pt and oxygen-rich MOF surface, which may promote the effective electron transfer and thereby improving the electrocatalytic performance [28,44]. As shown in Fig. 2F, the spin-orbital splitting of Pt \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) and Pt \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) could be fitted into two pairs of peaks (71.4 and \(74.4~\mathrm{eV}\) , 72.1 and \(76.3~\mathrm{eV}\) ), which are assigned to the dominant metallic Pt (0) and handful of Pt (II) states, respectively. With respect to the \(\mathrm{Pt}/\) C sample shown in Fig. S2, the deconvoluted peaks of Pt/MIL-100 (Fe) shifts by ca. \(0.3–0.4~\mathrm{eV}\) , further verifying the charge transfer and the interaction between Pt nanoclusters and MIL-100(Fe) [45,46].
## 2.2. HER performance of Pt/MIL-100(Fe)
From abovementioned lines, the electric interaction of Pt nanoclusters embedded MIL-100(Fe) may promote the electron transfer and proton-electron coupling, facilitating the quick release of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) , and thereby improving the HER performance [44]. The electrocatalytic HER activity of the Pt/MIL-100(Fe) electrocatalyst was measured via linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) in both acidic \((0.5\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})\) and alkaline (1.0 M KOH) electrolyte (Fig. 3). Overpotential \((\mathfrak{n}\ @\ 10\ \mathrm{mA\cm}^{-2})\) and Tafel slope are the two essential parameters for evaluating the HER performance, which could be acquired from the polarization curve [47,48]. In the acidic electrolyte, superior catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) is achieved as expect (Fig. 3A), which displays a lower onset potential and overpotential \((46\,\mathrm{mV})\) than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((63\;\mathrm{mV})\) . More importantly, the corresponding Tafel slope is only \(19.7\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) , rather close to \(20.3\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) acquired by \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) under the same conditions (Fig. 3B), revealing that the rate-determining step occurs at the end of the multielectron transfer and the excellent electrocatalytic performance. The Nyquist plot of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) obtained from electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is utilized to elucidate the charge transfer efficiency between Pt nanoclusters and supporting materials. As shown in Fig. 3C, it is noted that
the diameter of impedance arc (DIA) of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) modified electrode is obviously smaller than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) , suggesting the rapid transport of interfacial charge. The corresponding equivalent circuit fitted the impedance spectra R(QR), whereas \(\mathrm{R_{s}}\) expresses electrolyte resistance, \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) represents the chargetransfer resistance and \(\mathrm{Q_{s}}\) shows the constant phase elements (CPE). Moreover, the smaller \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) \((13.6\ \Omega)\) from the fitting curve compared to \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\left(67.2\;\Omega\right)\) indicates the more efficacious electron transfer attributed to interfacial interaction between Pt nanoclusters and MIL-100(Fe). As depicted in Fig. 3D, the polarization curves of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) after continuous cycling cyclic voltammetry (CVs) exhibit similar performance with negligible loss compared to initial state, which confirm the satisfactory catalytic stability in acidic electrolyte.
To verify the HER performance of Pt/MIL-100(Fe) in a universal pH solution, the measurements were also performed in alkaline electrolyte (Fig. 3D-G). It is noted that the overpotential \((29\ \mathrm{mV})\) and Tafel slope \((37.8\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) are rather lower with respect to commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((46\;\mathrm{mV},\,61.6\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) , and the \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) \((19.6\;\Omega)\) is also smaller than \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\left(59.2\;\Omega\right)\) , indicating the superior HER performance than \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) also in alkaline environment. However, the \(\boldsymbol\upeta\) and current density are declined after cycling CVs test, suggesting relatively worse durability in KOH solution, which is attributed to the hydrogen ionization in alkaline electrolyte is relatively difficult. Moreover, as depicted in Fig. S3, pure MIL-100(Fe) has high overpotential \((547/596\;\mathrm{mV}\) in acidic/alkaline electrolyte) and large Tafel slope \((246.9/106.8\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in acidic/alkaline electrolyte), which could be deemed as electrochemically inert for HER. The overpotential and the current density (at \(-100\ \mathrm{mV})\) are summarized in Fig. 3H for comparison, it is observed that the Pt/MIL100(Fe) modified electrode owns lower \(\boldsymbol\upeta\) and higher current density compared to commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) both in acidic and alkaline environment. Impressively, the above results suggest the excellent HER performance of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) , which is comparable with respect to the previously reported Pt-based HER electrocatalysts (Fig. 3I, Fig. S4) [28,35,48–51]. Electrocatalytic hydrogen production is a process that the electron transfer occurred on the surface/interior of catalyst electrode and promote the combination of transferred electron and the proton to release \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) [52,53]. HER mechanism is the adsorption and desorption hydrogen on the electrocatalyst sur
face, involving Volmer-Heyrovsky and Volmer-Tafel mechanism, and the possible reaction steps for HER are described in SI. To further understand the reasons contributed to the excellent HER performance, the primary aspects are expounded as below: Firstly, the geometric and porous Fe-MOF can enhance the Pt utilization efficiency and the closely packed Pt nanoclusters could maximize the Pt active sites, which facilitates the adsorption/desorption process during the electrocatalysis process. Then, the Gibbs free energy for proton adsorption of exposed \(f_{cc}\) Pt (111) crystal plane in Pt/MIL-100(Fe) is close to zero, which promotes the outstanding electrocatalytic activity for HER. Moreover, the poor conductivity of Fe-based MOF could be improved through the high conductivity of this Pt (111) crystal plane, thereby constructing the metal-MOF interaction, and facilitating the efficacious charge transfer. Ultimately, the available oxygen sites on the surface of MIL-100(Fe) would form Pt-O interaction with Pt nanoclusters to acquire desirable metal-supports interfacial interaction, which would boost the coupling of proton and electron, accelerating the release of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), thereby improving the HER performance.
## 2.3. EGOR properties of Pt/MIL-100(Fe)
To broad the proof-of-concept application of Pt nanoclusters embedded MIL-100(Fe), the ethylene glycol oxidation performance is also measured via cycling CVs, LSV, chronoamperometries (CA), chronopotentiometry (CP), EIS, etc. Commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) is acknowledged as an efficient electrocatalyst for alcohol oxidation reaction and it is adopted as the reference [54,55]. Fig. 4A displays the typical electro-oxidation peaks of ethylene glycol in the forward and backward curve located at ca. \(-0.2\mathrm{~V~}\) and \(-0.35\mathrm{~V~}\) attributed to
the oxidation of produced intermediates during the EGOR process. Firstly, the EGOR onset potential of Pt/MIL-100(Fe) electrode exhibits an evident negative shift compared to \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\), suggesting the better electrocatalytic activity of Pt/MIL-100(Fe). More importantly, the forward peak current density is employed to evaluate the mass catalytic activity for EGOR. From the 10th cycle of CVs, the mass electrocatalytic activity of commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) is \(3353\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}\). It is noted that the Pt/MIL-100(Fe) reaches the amazing value of \(11826\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}\), which is 3.53 times as much as \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) and obviously higher than recently reported Pt-based electrocatalysts (Table S1). As for pure MIL-100(Fe), no characteristic peak is observed in CV curve and it could be deemed as electrochemically inert for EGOR. Apart from the catalytic activity, the long-term durability and anti-poisoning ability are another essential index to assess the practicability of electrocatalysts [56]. Accordingly, the successive sweeping CVs of Pt/MIL-100(Fe) were carried out, as depicted in Fig. 4B-D. For the case for \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrode, the peak current density decays quickly over cycle numbers, which is attributed to the dissolution and poisoning of electrocatalyst during the EGOR process. Different from the reference, the Pt/MIL-100(Fe) presents a tendency that first increases and then decreases and reaches the highest current density at 30th cycle. After continuous 200 cycles, the Pt/MIL-100(Fe) electrode remains the high current density of \(7584\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}\) and the current percentage of \(64.13\%\), whereas the current percentage of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) decreases to \(49.48\%\) (a current density of \(1704\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}\)), indicating the superior cycling stability of Pt/MIL-100(Fe) than commercial one. To further verify the anti-poisoning ability of electrocatalysts, CA and CP experiments were measured. As depicted in Fig. 4C, the current density of two specimens firstly exhibits a rapid descent tendency ascribed to the inevitably poisoning by inter
mediates and the reduction of active sites. Following the initial stage, the mass activity achieves a steady state. After \(3000\ s\), the maintained mass activity of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) is \(558\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1}\), which is much larger than \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) one \((18\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pt}}^{-1})\), suggesting the better anti-poisoning capacity for Pt-MOF electrode. For the case of CP test (Fig. 4D), with the lengthened polarization time, the potential firstly climbs gradually and then leaps abruptly to a high potential attributed to the electrocatalysts poisoning and dissolving. Thus, the longer sustaining time means the superior anti-poisoning ability of electrocatalysts. It is noted that the sustaining time of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) reaches \(15790\ s\), which is approximately 2.47 times longer than those of \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) (6385 s). These measurements demonstrated that the as-prepared \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) owns the superior durability and anti-poisoning ability than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\), which is due to the stabilization effect of MOF-based supporting material and strong interfacial interaction of \(\mathrm{Pt}\) nanoclusters and \(\mathrm{MIL-100(Fe)}\). Additionally, to understand the charge mobility performance of electrocatalysts, the LSV tests and the corresponding Tafel slope were acquired and shown in Fig. S6 and Fig. 4E, respectively. LSV curves exhibit that the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) electrode lower onset potential and higher current density than \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) one, which is consistent with the abovementioned CVs. Meanwhile, the Tafel slopes of electrocatalysts obtained from the Tafel equation and fitted curves. Alike with HER, smaller Tafel slope signifies faster electrocatalytic reaction rate. It is noted that the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) \((62\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) owns smaller Tafel slopes compared to \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) \((83\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\), revealing the faster kinetic rate of Pt-MOF for EGOR. Furthermore, the EIS measurement of two specimens for EGOR at \(-0.25\;\mathrm{V}\) was also performed to study charge-transfer efficiency. As shown in Fig. 4F, it is observed that the DIA of \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) is evidently smaller than \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) samples, implying the more efficacious charge transfer for \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) than control one. Moreover, the equivalent circuit fit of the EIS spectra is also the R(QR) and the smaller interface charge-transfer resistance \(\left(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)\) means the effective charge transfer. As expected, the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100}\) (Fe) \((33.71\;\Omega)\) exhibits the smaller \(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\) value than that of \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) \((86.98\;\Omega)\). To further investigate the electron transfer kinetics of EGOR on the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) electrode, the EIS at various potentials ranged \(-0.45\;\mathrm{V}\) to \(0\;\mathrm{V}\) are conducted (Fig. S7). With the continuous increment of potential from \(-0.45\;\mathrm{V}\) to \(-0.30\;\mathrm{V}\), the DIA obviously decreased, implying the incremental available active sites due to the oxidation removal of generated CO intermediate species at higher potentials. As depicted in Fig. S7, from \(-0.30\;\mathrm{V}\) to \(-0.15\;\mathrm{V}\),
the smallest DIA at \(-0.25\;\mathrm{V}\) reveals the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) modified electrode has superior electron transfer at this potential. Interestingly, the impedance arcs suddenly reversed to the second quadrant at \(-0.10\) and \(-0.05\;\mathrm{V}\), which is attributed to the oxidation removal of CO-like intermediate from the electrocatalyst surface, leading to the restoration of catalytic active sites. Moreover, the arcs returned to the first quadrant with a large DIA at \(0\;\mathrm{V}\) due to the absence of intermediate species. The above results confirms that the Pt nanoclusters embedded MOF displays superior catalytic activity and durability for EGOR than \(\mathrm{Pt/C}\) owing to the efficacious charge transfer and desirable interfacial interaction between \(\mathrm{Pt}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-100(Fe)}\). For the electrocatalytic mechanism of EGOR, the Pt nanoclusters embedded on MOF acted as the dominating active sites during the electrocatalytic process. As described pathways of EGOR in SI, the electro-oxidation of ethylene glycol yields to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) on the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) catalyst surface via the direct pathway (oxidization of the \(-\mathrm{HCO}\), \(-\mathrm{HCOO}\), etc.) and the indirect way (oxidization of \(\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}\)).
## 3. Conclusion
Rational design and convenient fabrication of high-efficiency and durable electrocatalyst are rather essential for multifunctional clean energy electrocatalytic reaction. In this study, a novel Pt nanocluster embedded \(\mathrm{MIL-100(Fe)}\) were designed and prepared via a facile modified hydrothermal method and served as an efficient and durable dual-functional electrocatalyst for HER and EGOR. Considering the nanostructure, nanoscale and face plane of Pt would play the dominant role in the electrocatalytic performance of Pt-based catalyst, the \(\mathrm{Pt/MIL-100(Fe)}\) electrocatalyst with closely packed Pt nanoclusters could maximize the Pt active sites to facilitate the adsorption/desorption process for electrochemical reaction and the exposed (111) face plane of Pt has the Gibbs free energy close to zero could accelerate the catalytic kinetics. More importantly, the low-cost and low toxicity Fe-based MOF with available oxygen sites could form Pt-O bonding with Pt nanoclusters to boost the coupling of proton and electron and accelerate the catalytic kinetics. Furthermore, the Pt nanoclusters could improve the conductivity of \(\mathrm{MIL-100(Fe)}\) in turn and thereby enhance the effective charge transfer. As expected, excellent HER performance with rather low overpotential \((46/29\;\mathrm{mV}\) in acidic/alkaline electrolyte) and small Tafel slope \((119.7/37.8\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in acidic/alkaline electrolyte) both in acid
and alkaline environment are acquired in the Pt/MIL-100(Fe) electrode. Moreover, the mass electrocatalytic activity of Pt/MIL-100 (Fe) for EGOR reaches the amazing value of \(11826\ \mathrm{mA\ mg^{-1}}\). The satisfactory HER and EGOR performances of Pt/MIL-100(Fe) are evidently superior than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) and is comparable with respect to recently reported Pt-based electrocatalysts. In summary, compared with conventional Pt-based electrocatalyst, the proposed Pt/MIL-100(Fe) not only combined the merits of Pt nanoclusters and MOF, but also effectively established the strong interaction via metal-oxygen bonding to acquire excellent catalytic performance for dual-functional hydrogen evolution and alcohols oxidation reaction. The present investigation demonstrates the promise of the combination of active metal and MOF for the interfacial strategy and rational design of dual-functional electrocatalyst, we do believe that this work may drive more researchers to explore multifunctional catalysts for clean energy electrochemical reaction and sheds light into the mechanism related to the synergistic effect of active metal and MOF.
